# Layer 2 — Full Document: SL-ARCH
# SIGN Language — Parser & Toolchain Architecture

@doc SL-ARCH [std, active, agents+employees] v1 sha:pending
@links SL-OVERVIEW,SL-DEPS,SL-STANDARDS

@anchor [repo:sign-lang, vcs:git]
  + validator/src/**
  + validator/bin/**
  + validator/test/**
  + editor-support/**
  ! validator/test/fixtures/**

@vocab
  parses        | source module reads .sign text and produces structured document objects
  validates     | rule engine checks structural and semantic constraints against parsed output
  compiles      | compiler transforms validated source into Layer 1 index + Layer 2 bundle
  measures      | coverage module resolves anchor patterns against a repository file tree
  detects       | drift module identifies source changes not reflected in governing documents
  classifies    | diff module categorises document changes as additive, coverage-narrowing, or meaning-breaking

@def sign-toolchain
  Regex-based validator and compiler for SIGN v1.0 documents. The toolchain is
  dependency-free (zero npm dependencies) and runs on Node.js >=18. It provides six
  CLI commands: validate, compile, coverage, drift, diff, and import. The parser
  produces structured document objects (not an AST); an AST-producing parser is
  deferred to phase 4 alongside LSP support (ADR-0001).

@props
  + zero-dependencies    | no npm dependencies; Node.js built-ins only (fs, path, crypto, child_process)
  + regex-based-parser   | line-oriented regex parsing; no AST; structured ParsedDoc objects
  + canonical-block-order| spec section 4.2 defines sigil ordering; parser and rules enforce it
  + six-cli-commands     | validate, compile, coverage, drift, diff, import
  + layer-1-compiler     | emits canon-index.sign with ~400 token budget enforcement
  + layer-2-compiler     | emits canon-bundle.sign preserving exact authoring (raw file passthrough)
  + integrity-artifacts  | canon-manifest.json (version, sha, doc count) + .sha256 checksum
  + anchor-coverage      | glob-to-regexp resolution of @anchor patterns against repository trees
  + drift-detection      | git-based detection of source changes under anchored documents
  + diff-classification  | meaning-breaking / coverage-narrowing / additive change classification
  + import-delegation    | sign import delegates to @sign-tools/sign-ingest (discovered, not imported)

@rules
  1. Block order: blocks within a document follow canonical order (spec section 4.2):
     doc, links, anchor, cycle, reviewed, vocab, def, props, include, exclude, rules,
     boundary, constraints, cluster, infer, rel, edges, xwalk, attrs, disallowed, phases,
     status, reserved
  2. Vocab completeness: every predicate used in @rel (outbound/->) or @edges must be
     declared in @vocab within the same document
  3. Infer completeness: @infer blocks require when:, then:, confidence:, and src: fields
  4. Reference resolution: @links targets must resolve to known document IDs; @cluster
     and rule: references in @edges must resolve to declared @cluster/@infer blocks
  5. Symmetric edge bidirectionality: predicates declared <=> must appear in both
     directions across the document set (bundle-level check)
  6. Index budget: Layer 1 index must not exceed ~400 tokens (chars/4 approximation)
  7. Status enforcement: active documents must not @link to inactive/deprecated documents
  8. Anchor coverage (ANCHOR001-005): anchor must govern at least one artifact; no overlap
     across documents; exclusions must intersect includes; compact form is Layer-1 only;
     OKF-sourced anchors require @xwalk provenance

@constraints
  sign-toolchain
  requires: Node.js >= 18.0.0
  requires: git checkout for drift and diff commands
  forbids: external npm dependencies in validator package
  forbids: AST construction in current parser (deferred to phase 4)
  enforced-by: validator rule engine (runAllRules) and CI
